{% set local_problem %}
<p>Here's a reality check for Madison homeowners: many homes built in the last 10 years are now due for a gutter assessment. Those builder-grade sectional gutters that came with your house? They were chosen because they were cheap, not because they were right for North Alabama's climate.</p>

<p>Madison's rapid growth through the 2000s and 2010s meant homes went up fast. Subdivisions near County Line Road, along Highway 72, and throughout the Dublin Park area were built during a construction boom when builders often cut corners on gutters. The result is a generation of Madison homes with undersized, poorly installed gutter systems that are now starting to fail — leaking at seams, pulling away from fascia boards, and overflowing during every moderate rainstorm.</p>

<p>Combine those failing gutters with Madison County's infamous red clay soil (which doesn't absorb water) and the region's 55+ inches of annual rainfall, and you've got a recipe for foundation problems, landscape erosion, and water damage. Seamless gutters solve this at the source — no seams means no leaks, and custom fabrication means gutters that actually fit your home and handle the water volume you need.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Seamless gutters are exactly what they sound like: continuous gutters with no seams except at corners and downspout connections. Unlike traditional sectional gutters (which are made of pre-cut 10-foot sections joined together), seamless gutters are fabricated on-site from a single piece of aluminum coil — custom-cut to the exact length of each run on your home.</p>

<p>Why does this matter? Because seams are where gutters fail. Every joint in a sectional gutter system is a potential leak point, a place for debris to catch, and a weak spot that can separate over time. Seamless gutters eliminate these problems entirely. The result is a cleaner look, better performance, and dramatically longer lifespan.</p>

<p>We bring our portable gutter machine directly to your Madison home, feed in aluminum coil, and fabricate your gutters right in your driveway. What comes out is a precision-fit gutter system designed specifically for your roofline — not generic sections forced to fit.</p>
{% endset %}

